  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nA complex low pressure system will bring rain and wind to the Ohio\nValley over the next day or so. The main drivers of the weather will\nbe a surface low moving north along the east coast and into\nPennsylvania, as well as an upper trough (and closed low) drifting\neast through the middle Mississippi Valley and Tennessee Valley\nregions. Over the Ohio Valley, theta-e advection aloft is already\noccurring, though the dry air and northeasterly flow near the surface\nhas meant that precipitation is still not yet reaching the ground in\nthe ILN CWA. This will change quickly as the column saturates over\nthe next few hours, leading to rain for most of the day as the region\nremains near a broad band of forcing to the northwest of the surface\nlow, and north of the upper low. This looks like a high-confidence\nwidespread inch of rain for the entire ILN CWA, with some pockets\ngetting up to an inch and a half. There is a lower probability of up\nto two inches in some spots, but most locations will probably receive\n1.0 - 1.5 inches of rain.\n\nThe wind forecast is somewhat difficult. There will be a tight\npressure gradient and relatively strong sustained ENE winds today,\nperhaps as much as 20 MPH. The potential for gusts appears to be\nthere, with BUFKIT soundings from several models suggesting a very\nsmall mixed layer that may slightly help with mixing some of the\nstronger winds aloft to the surface. This forecast is similar to the\nprevious forecast, with some gusts in the 30-40 MPH range possible,\nparticularly in the northern half of the ILN CWA. Some raw model \ngust output may be overdoing it given the presence of the inversion,\nwhich does happen at times in scenarios like this.\n\nThere remains a chance of some light snow mixing in on Friday\nmorning, though this will be dependent on how cold the boundary layer\nis able to get. Though this part of the forecast is not particularly\nhigh confidence, no accumulations or impacts are expected.\n\nIt will not be until Friday morning that the upper low will be moving\naway from the area. Height rises will lead to improving weather\nconditions, with precipitation coming to an end, and some clearing \nof sky cover.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nRidging will provide a brief period of dry weather late Friday\nthrough Saturday, before the upper pattern flattens out and becomes\nmore progressive again. There is high confidence that another\nshortwave will move through the region some time Sunday, bringing\nrain and another cold front through the area. Exact timing and\nrainfall amounts are lower in confidence for now, and may be\ninfluenced by whether or not a surface low develops along the cold\nfront and ahead of the shortwave.\n\nCooler air will move in behind the cold front for Monday, but drier\nconditions and a warming trend are expected heading into the middle\nof the week.",
